WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.186
Windows forensics depends on correlating user activity, logs, registry artifacts, file system evidence, execution traces, and security telemetry.

00:00:09.186 --> 00:00:13.661
User profiles can show activity under an account.

00:00:13.661 --> 00:00:19.549
Event logs can show authentication, services, security events, and application behavior.

00:00:19.549 --> 00:00:25.437
Registry hives can reveal configuration, autoruns, device history, and user-specific artifacts.

00:00:25.437 --> 00:00:30.383
File system evidence can support timelines and execution analysis.

00:00:30.383 --> 00:00:34.387
Strong analysis preserves evidence and avoids overclaiming.

00:00:34.387 --> 00:00:46.870
A jump list entry, logon event, registry key, Prefetch artifact, scheduled task, or security alert may be important, but it is rarely enough by itself.

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:54.642
Analysts should compare multiple sources, check timestamps, understand system context, and document gaps or uncertainty.

00:00:54.642 --> 00:00:57.233
Collection choices shape confidence.

00:00:57.233 --> 00:01:11.129
Full disk images, targeted collections, memory capture, log exports, registry exports, EDR telemetry, hashes, chain of custody records, and case notes all help make the work more defensible.

00:01:11.129 --> 00:01:20.315
The analyst should explain what was collected, what was not collected, and how those choices affect the conclusions.

00:01:20.315 --> 00:01:23.377
The goal is defensible understanding.

00:01:23.377 --> 00:01:36.802
Windows forensics supports incident response, insider investigations, malware triage, and user activity reconstruction by answering what happened, when it happened, and what systems or accounts were affected.

00:01:36.802 --> 00:01:45.045
When the analysis is careful and evidence-based, it helps the organization make better decisions under pressure.
